WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.600
Horror movies have been freaking out audiences
for over 100 years.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:09.000
But it turns out that once you take away the
spooky lighting, ominous music, and scary

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.480
masks, a terrifying spectacle can easily become
nothing more than just a bunch of people having

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:13.980
fun.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:18.100
Here are some amusingly disillusioning set
photos that will keep you from looking at

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.640
iconic horror movies the same way ever again.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.020
Freddy Krueger is easily the most charismatic
killer of the '80s slasher era.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.560
As the Nightmare on Elm Street franchise expanded,
Freddy became quippier and punnier until he

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.200
basically turned into the '80s version of
a slightly-less-murdery Deadpool without a

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:36.700
mask.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.740
But before he became such a cuddly figure,
he was a serial killer with wizard powers

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.020
who was probably also a child abuser.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:47.860
That's why it's kind of jarring to see him
chilling with the teens he would dream-murder

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.880
while bumping 1984's biggest hits on a Walkman.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.120
Not even a living nightmare could resist taking
a break with Stevie Wonder's "I Just Called

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.120
to Say I Love You."

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.460
By tearing away the curtain of fantasy, these
behind-the-scenes photos remind you that these

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:06.399
monsters are just puppets or people in makeup,
breaking the illusion and removing most of

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:06.900
the horror.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.959
The problem with this shot from behind the
scenes of 1990's Gremlins 2: The New Batch

00:01:11.959 --> 00:01:14.930
is that it's actually way scarier than anything
in the movie.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:26.280
The adorable mogwai Gizmo is arguably the
hero and definitely the breakout star of the

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.260
Gremlins franchise, so he's generally meant
to be cute and tiny rather than scary.

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:36.380
But makeup artist Rick Baker's team built
a prototype human-sized suit for a small actor

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.340
to fit into so Gizmo could walk around on
camera.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.659
Thankfully, this idea was ditched and this
horrifying, presumably child-eating suit never

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:45.720
appeared on camera.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.840
Thirsty Lady Gremlin was scary enough.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:54.400
2006's Pan's Labyrinth was arguably the film
that made director Guillermo del Toro a household

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:54.900
name.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:59.920
The film uses dark fantasy to create a dream
world in which the horrors of fascist Spain

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.800
are reflected by the horrors of a big maze
with monsters in it.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:08.179
The monster who gives his name to the title
is the Faun, but the real nightmare star is

00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:13.150
the Pale Man, whose "eyeballs in the palms"
bit is probably the film's most memorable

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:13.640
visual.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:17.920
The actor behind the Faun would probably be
mad that the Pale Man upstaged him if it weren't

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.900
for the fact that they were played by the
same actor: Doug Jones.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.420
Jones is reportedly a very nice person despite
his monstrous roles, so it's no surprise to

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:29.540
see him showing some affection behind the
scenes to his makeup artist.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:36.120
1974's The Texas Chain Saw Massacre is one
of the most influential and beloved horror

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.200
films ever made.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:43.700
1986's Texas Chainsaw Massacre 2 has something
less of a critical consensus behind it, but

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.600
its reputation has grown in recent years.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.240
Director Tobe Hooper wanted the sequel to
expand on the black comedy elements of the

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.780
first film, so it really amps up the satire
as well as the gore to make something almo

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.120
st as over the top as the first film is understated.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:05.180
That spirit of fun really shows in this behind-the-scenes
photo, in which Leatherface is playing cards

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:10.379
with his brothers the Cook and Chop Top in
a game that seems to have gone somewhat awry…

00:03:10.379 --> 00:03:12.650
but far less awry than their usual games.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:18.499
The Hellraiser franchise is about demons from
another dimension in leather gear with elaborate

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.680
body mods that steal people's souls.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:25.820
Easily the most recognizable character is
Pinhead, a leader of the Cenobites, portrayed

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:27.960
in the first seven films by Doug Bradley.

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:33.840
According to an interview with Birth Movies
Death, while promoting Hellraiser III in 1992,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.160
Bradley was walking through New York in full
costume so he could present a prop to Planet

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:38.660
Hollywood.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:44.620
Naturally, his presence drew quite a crowd,
and at some point one of the dozens of people

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:48.500
following him down the street asked him to
take a picture holding a baby.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.720
Bradley refers to this as "Pinhead running
for President."

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.760
Wes Craven's A Nightmare on Elm Street ended
up exploding into a massive franchise, and

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:03.000
pretty much everyone agrees that out of all
the sequels, the best one is part 3, The Dream

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:03.500
Warriors.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.159
One of the most important additions to the
Elm Street mythos in this film is the role

00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:13.959
of Kristen Parker, portrayed by Patricia Arquette,
who has the ability to summon other people

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:15.800
into the dream world at will.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:20.440
And one of her most famous scenes is the one
in which Freddy appears as a giant snake that

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:25.110
tries to swallow Kristen whole until he's
stabbed with a shard of glass and pukes her

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:25.600
up.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.960
As cool as that scene is, it's somewhat undercut
by this shot of Arquette not at all bothered

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.540
to be halfway down Freddy's gullet.

